body:
`applyPins()` always creates a new `children` array via `.map()`, so `children !== item.children` will be true for any item with children. This causes every group node to be cloned on every call even when nothing is pinned, defeating the referential equality optimization and potentially triggering extra re-renders. Consider only allocating a new `children` array when a child actually changes (track a `changed` flag while mapping) and return the original item/children when no updates are needed.
```suggestion
      let itemsChanged = false;

      const nextItems = items.map(item => {
        const pinned = pinnedIds.includes(item.id);

        let children = item.children;
        let childrenChanged = false;

        if (item.children?.length) {
          const nextChildren = applyPins(item.children);
          if (nextChildren !== item.children) {
            children = nextChildren;
            childrenChanged = true;
          }
        }

        const pinnedChanged = pinned !== (item.pinned ?? false);

        if (pinnedChanged || childrenChanged) {
          itemsChanged = true;
          return { ...item, pinned, children };
        }

        return item;
      });

      return itemsChanged ? nextItems : items;
```
